  "about": "Whether a person serving a felony prison sentence may vote, by place. Maine, Vermont and the District of Columbia never take the vote for a conviction. Puerto Rico places no voting restriction on a conviction. In the other 48 states and in Guam, American Samoa, the Northern Mariana Islands and the U.S. Virgin Islands a person serving a felony prison sentence may not vote while serving. Whether the vote returns on release, after parole or after probation varies and is not modeled.",
  "source": "National Conference of State Legislatures, Felon Voting Rights (https://www.ncsl.org/research/elections-and-campaigns/felon-voting-rights.aspx), read Oct. 15, 2026: the District of Columbia, Maine and Vermont never remove the vote. Puerto Rico: reported by the Campaign Legal Center as having no conviction-based restriction (read through a secondary report, not the Center's own page). Territories: Vote.gov, Voting after a felony conviction (https://vote.gov/guide-to-voting/after-felony-conviction), and U.S. Vote Foundation pages for each territory. The check against each statute was not made.",
